# =============================================================================
# #3838 — the roles, in a file, for the first time.
#
# Until now chorus:role-wren and its siblings existed ONLY in the live store.
# Every ownership edge in the model points at them — holdsRole, ownedBy on
# products and domains and cards — and none of them could be reproduced by a
# deploy. Silas's CSS extraction (#3837) is blocked on exactly this: re-seed
# identity and the Principal→Role edges dangle against individuals nobody can
# rebuild.
#
# ONE SPELLING. There were three:
#   chorus:wren        AgentRole, urn:chorus:ontology  — 72 references, but
#                      nothing at runtime points at it
#   chorus:role-wren   Role, urn:chorus:instances      — 6 references, and they
#                      are holdsRole + every ownedBy
#   chorus:wren-owner  Role, test fixture only
#
# chorus:role-* wins, and not by preference: `athena-model add --kind role`
# mints exactly that IRI, so the governed writer had already decided. The other
# two are retired by this card rather than aliased — an owl:sameAs bridge is how
# three spellings became normal in the first place.
#
# The graph is urn:chorus:instances (pure ABox, ADR-025), loaded through the
# INSTANCE_SET leg of chorus-model-deploy, which is purely additive and cannot
# delete a co-tenant.
#
# NOTE ON TYPES: each role is typed BOTH chorus:Role and its subclass. Our store
# does no inference, so a role typed only AgentRole would vanish from every
# query that asks for a Role — and every ownership constraint in the model asks
# for a Role. Dual-typing is deliberate, not belt-and-braces: it is the only way
# the subclass carries meaning without breaking the superclass queries. This
# also retires the string "kind=agent" living inside a comment field, which is
# what the type system existed to express.
# =============================================================================

@prefix chorus: <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#> .
@prefix rdfs:   <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .

chorus:role-jeff a chorus:Role, chorus:HumanRole ;
    chorus:roleKind "human" ;
    rdfs:label "Jeff" ; chorus:label "Jeff" ;
    rdfs:comment "Owner. Judgment, direction, meaning. The authority the roles serve — holds no Principal→Role edge, deliberately." ;
    chorus:comment "Owner. Judgment, direction, meaning." ;
    chorus:rolePriority 0 .

chorus:role-wren a chorus:Role, chorus:AgentRole ;
    chorus:roleKind "agent" ;
    rdfs:label "Wren" ; chorus:label "Wren" ;
    rdfs:comment "Product Manager. Owns the interaction layer — Clearing, Athena, cards, roles. Value first, ship small, have a position." ;
    chorus:comment "Product Manager. Value first, ship small, have a position." ;
    chorus:rolePriority 3 .

chorus:role-silas a chorus:Role, chorus:AgentRole ;
    chorus:roleKind "agent" ;
    rdfs:label "Silas" ; chorus:label "Silas" ;
    rdfs:comment "Architect. Owns the observation layer and identity — dashboards, alerting, gates, the security model and the authN seam." ;
    chorus:comment "Architect. Observation layer, identity, authN." ;
    chorus:rolePriority 2 .

# #3860/#3985 — the nightly runs as its OWN role, not borrowed agent identity.
# The write door resolves agent_id from holdsRole; principal-nightly had
# identity + scope but no role, so every batch write 403'd and the test ledger
# stored nothing. A distinct role keeps nightly writes attributed as "nightly"
# — reusing an agent role would let automation masquerade as a person, the
# exact self-attribution hole #3651 closed. Plain chorus:Role, deliberately:
# no new subclass minted for one member.
chorus:role-nightly a chorus:Role ;
    rdfs:label "nightly" ; chorus:label "nightly" ;
    chorus:roleKind "automation" ;
    chorus:comment "The 03:00 automation identity — runs the suites, posts the results. Least-privilege principal (#3975); writes attributed to nightly, never to an agent." .

chorus:role-kade a chorus:Role, chorus:AgentRole ;
    chorus:roleKind "agent" ;
    rdfs:label "Kade" ; chorus:label "Kade" ;
    rdfs:comment "Engineer. Owns the build and delivery layer — werk verbs, tests, the pipeline, presentation." ;
    chorus:comment "Engineer. Build and delivery, werk, tests." ;
    chorus:rolePriority 1 .

# #3876 — the hasProperty edges live HERE, not in chorus.ttl.
#
# The rule, settled with Silas 2026-08-14: an EDGE lives in the home graph
# of the node it hangs off. RoleShape pins Role individuals to
# urn:chorus:instances, so a role's hasProperty edge belongs in this file.
#
# They were authored into chorus.ttl (ontology) alongside the Property
# individuals, which is where the Properties correctly live — fields with
# their subject. The split meant /effective found a typed Role with no
# properties and answered 404, and Jeff's word cap stayed a compiled
# constant for two days after every piece of the path existed.
#
# gate.enforced looked like counter-evidence (property, edge and node all
# in ontology) but ServiceShape pins Service to ontology, so it satisfies
# edge-follows-node too. The rules coincide there; it proved nothing.
chorus:role-silas chorus:hasProperty chorus:prop-role-silas-response-word-cap .
chorus:role-wren chorus:hasProperty chorus:prop-role-wren-response-word-cap .
chorus:role-kade chorus:hasProperty chorus:prop-role-kade-response-word-cap .
chorus:role-silas chorus:hasProperty chorus:prop-role-silas-nudge-word-cap .
chorus:role-wren chorus:hasProperty chorus:prop-role-wren-nudge-word-cap .
chorus:role-kade chorus:hasProperty chorus:prop-role-kade-nudge-word-cap .
